{"retractions" [[17592186379305 :question/problem "Given that $L{{e}^{at}}=\\frac{1}{s-a}$ for $s>a$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-3τ}\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>-3.$"] [17592186379305 :question/worked-solution "We know that if $L{f(t)}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)={e}^{-3t}.$ Thus, for $s>-3,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L{f(t)} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{-3t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s>-3,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t)} & =L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-3τ}\\,\\text{d}τ} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s(s+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186379307 17592186379308 17592186379309 17592186379310 17592186379311] "immutable_fields" [[17592186379306 :answer-field/id] [17592186379306 :answer-field/key] [17592186379306 :answer-field/type] [17592186379306 :answer-field/correct] [17592186379306 :answer-field/choices]]}
